    "bot": false,
    "body": "We run thumbor in production and this bug affects one of our users, so we dug deeper into the root cause — and it turns out it's **not a transparency bug**, which means #1774 won't fix it.\n\n**Root cause:** in `thumbor/transformer.py`, `auto_crop()` computes\n\n```python\ncrop_height = int(round(source_width * self.target_height / target_width, 0))\n```\n\nFor a 1x1 source resized to 50x25 this gives `round(0.5) = 0` (Python banker's rounding), so the image is cropped to `(0, 0, 1, 0)` — an empty 1x0 image. PIL's `resize()` on an empty image produces all-`(0,0,0,0)` pixels, which is exactly the \"image disappears\" symptom. 50x26 works because `round(0.52) = 1`. Any target ratio ≥ 2:1 on a 1x1 source triggers it.\n\n**Why #1774 doesn't help (and actually makes it worse):** the patched `resize()` calls `has_transparency()`, which does `min(self.image.convert(\"RGBA\").getchannel(\"A\").getextrema())`. On the empty cropped image `getextrema()` returns `None`, so the request now fails with a 500 (`TypeError: 'NoneType' object is not iterable`) instead of returning a wrong image. Reproduced on the `pr-1774` branch with Pillow 11.3.0.\n\n**Fix we validated** (clamping the crop to at least 1px, in `auto_crop()` right after the crop math):\n\n```python\ncrop_width = max(1, crop_width)\ncrop_height = max(1, crop_height)\n```\n\nWith this, every size we tested (50x25, 50x10, 30x15, 100x50, 500x1) returns the correct uniform RGBA pixel. Happy to open a PR with the fix + a regression test if that helps."
